---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import FileDiagram from '../components/FileDiagram.astro';
import WindowsDiagram from '../components/WindowsDiagram.astro';
import AttackerDiagram from '../components/AttackerDiagram.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
import Arrow from '../components/Arrow.astro';
---
<Layout title="File protection architecture">
  <PageHero eyebrow="THE TECHNOLOGY" title="Look inside the protection." text="Storage access is not file access. Readable data requires the correct protected fragments, protected cryptographic material and the authorized Cyber Reliant reconstruction path." number="04"/>
  <section class="section container" id="windows">
    <div class="section-heading windows-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">WINDOWS IMPLEMENTATION</span><h2>The filter doesn’t just intercept the file.<br/>It participates in rebuilding it.</h2></div><p>Cyber Reliant’s file-system filter participates in the information and processes required to turn protected fragments back into a readable file.</p></div>
    <WindowsDiagram/>
    <div class="attacker-section" id="attacker-path"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">A DIFFERENT DRIVER IS NOT THE SAME PROCESS</span><h2>Reaching storage<br/>doesn’t rebuild the file.</h2></div><p>Replacing or bypassing a conventional file-system driver does not, by itself, reproduce Cyber Reliant reconstruction.</p></div><AttackerDiagram/>
      <details class="technical-disclosure"><summary>Threat-model boundary: driver replacement and kernel-level attacks</summary><p>This illustration distinguishes storage access from the information, cryptographic material and protected processes needed for reconstruction. It does not establish that bypass is impossible or that Cyber Reliant is immune to kernel-level attack. Evaluate fragment identification, application identity, key access, memory exposure and reconstruction behavior against the intended threat model.</p></details>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="dark-section" id="network"><div class="container section network-detail">
    <div class="network-intro"><div><span class="outline-tag">UPCOMING NETWORK VERSION</span><h2>The data path and the key path<br/>are separate by design.</h2></div><div><p class="large-copy">The architecture fragments the cryptographic material itself.</p><p>Encrypted file fragments are distributed across logical storage locations. Separately, cryptographic material is fragmented or shredded, and multiple key components are protected through the HSM architecture. Both paths come together through authorized Cyber Reliant reconstruction.</p></div></div>
    <div class="network-visual"><FileDiagram compact/></div>
    <details class="technical-disclosure"><summary>Network architecture details and evaluation boundaries</summary><p>The illustration describes the upcoming network architecture. Component counts are illustrative; it does not specify a threshold scheme, an HSM topology or physically independent storage failure domains. HSM models, validation status, key-component lifecycle, recovery and availability must be confirmed for the intended release. Separation is not a guarantee that key material can never be recovered or that files can never be compromised.</p></details>
  </div></section>
  <section class="container section"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">SECURITY IS IN THE DETAILS</span><h2>Define the boundaries.<br/>Then test them.</h2></div></div><div class="technical-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Technical evaluation table, scroll horizontally for all columns"><table class="technical-table"><caption>Questions for a technical evaluation</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">Architecture</th><th scope="col">What to verify</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">File path</th><td>Fragment the file, encrypt its fragments and distribute the protected fragments</td><td>Algorithms, correct fragment identification, integrity controls, dependencies and logical storage boundaries</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Network key path <span class="table-status">Upcoming</span></th><td>Fragment/shred cryptographic material and protect multiple key components through the HSM architecture</td><td>Component protection, permitted operations, rotation, revocation, recovery and the HSM integration</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Authorized reconstruction</th><td>Bring required file fragments and protected cryptographic material together through the Cyber Reliant access path</td><td>Authorization, decryption, reconstruction behavior and readable-data exposure during use</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Windows filter</th><td>Participate in identifying fragments, obtaining cryptographic material, decrypting and rebuilding the file</td><td>Supported Windows versions, replacement-filter scenarios, application identity, kernel-level threat model and performance</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Operational resilience</th><td>Separate stored file fragments from the complete reconstruction path</td><td>Backup/restore behavior, fragment or key-component loss, storage outages and incident procedures</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div></section>
  <section class="soft-section"><div class="section container split-content"><div><span class="eyebrow">ZERO TRUST, PRECISELY</span><h2>Make the access<br/>decision explicit.</h2></div><div><p class="large-copy">Reconstruction belongs to an authorized access path—not every process that can reach storage.</p><p>Application-level controls govern which applications can proceed. Cyber Reliant then participates in identifying, decrypting and rebuilding the protected file.</p><a class="text-link" href="/request-demo?interest=Technical%20architecture">Discuss your trust boundaries <Arrow/></a><details class="technical-disclosure"><summary>Where this fits in a zero-trust architecture</summary><p>File protection is one part of a broader architecture. It does not replace identity, device, network or operational controls, or establish a complete zero-trust architecture by itself.</p></details></div></div></section>
  <CTA title="Go deeper than the diagram." text="Walk through the file path, the key path and the reconstruction process against your threat model."/>
</Layout>
